# Arch userland fragment — merged LAST, so it wins.
#
# THIS IS THE FRAGMENT ROCKNIX AND ARMADA DO NOT GIVE YOU, and the reason a
# straight copy of their defconfig is not sufficient.
#
# ROCKNIX is an immutable LibreELEC-lineage appliance: squashfs root, no package
# manager, no containers, a fixed set of services. Its config is tuned for that
# and legitimately omits most of what a general-purpose mutable Arch system
# needs. Armada is bootc/ostree, so it needs a different-but-also-not-ours set
# (composefs, ostree, dracut).
#
# Anything here that collides with 10-qcom-platform.config is a real conflict —
# merge_config.sh warns on override, and the build script fails on any requested
# =y that ends up unset. Read the warnings.

# ============== systemd / udev hard requirements ==============
CONFIG_DEVTMPFS=y
CONFIG_DEVTMPFS_MOUNT=y
CONFIG_CGROUPS=y
CONFIG_CGROUP_BPF=y
CONFIG_CGROUP_SCHED=y
CONFIG_CGROUP_FREEZER=y
CONFIG_CGROUP_PIDS=y
CONFIG_CGROUP_DEVICE=y
CONFIG_MEMCG=y
CONFIG_BLK_CGROUP=y
CONFIG_INOTIFY_USER=y
CONFIG_SIGNALFD=y
CONFIG_TIMERFD=y
CONFIG_EPOLL=y
CONFIG_NET=y
CONFIG_SYSFS=y
CONFIG_PROC_FS=y
CONFIG_FHANDLE=y
CONFIG_CRYPTO_USER_API_HASH=y
CONFIG_CRYPTO_HMAC=y
CONFIG_CRYPTO_SHA256=y
CONFIG_TMPFS=y
CONFIG_TMPFS_POSIX_ACL=y
CONFIG_TMPFS_XATTR=y
CONFIG_SECCOMP=y
CONFIG_SECCOMP_FILTER=y

# ============== namespaces — containers, flatpak, bwrap, Steam ==============
# Steam's pressure-vessel runtime and every flatpak use user namespaces.
CONFIG_NAMESPACES=y
CONFIG_USER_NS=y
CONFIG_PID_NS=y
CONFIG_NET_NS=y
CONFIG_UTS_NS=y
CONFIG_IPC_NS=y

# ============== filesystems Arch actually uses ==============
CONFIG_EXT4_FS=y
CONFIG_EXT4_FS_POSIX_ACL=y
CONFIG_EXT4_FS_SECURITY=y
CONFIG_VFAT_FS=y
CONFIG_FAT_DEFAULT_UTF8=y
CONFIG_NLS_CODEPAGE_437=y
CONFIG_NLS_ISO8859_1=y
CONFIG_NLS_UTF8=y
# =y, not =m: this is the ROOT filesystem, and profiles that boot without an
# initramfs (ayn-odin3, NEED_INITRAMFS=0) have nowhere to load a module from —
# it would live on the filesystem it is needed to mount. The image build asserts
# this against modules.builtin rather than trusting it; see arch-arm64
# build_files/20-install-kernel.sh.
#
# POSIX_ACL because the shared-data layout uses default ACLs on the group-owned
# trees, and btrfs silently ignores them without it.
CONFIG_BTRFS_FS=y
CONFIG_BTRFS_FS_POSIX_ACL=y
CONFIG_F2FS_FS=m
CONFIG_EXFAT_FS=m
CONFIG_NTFS3_FS=m
CONFIG_OVERLAY_FS=y
CONFIG_FUSE_FS=y
CONFIG_SQUASHFS=y
CONFIG_SQUASHFS_XZ=y
CONFIG_SQUASHFS_ZSTD=y
CONFIG_EROFS_FS=y

# ============== loadable modules ==============
# ROCKNIX can get away with a fully static kernel. Arch cannot — DKMS, out-of-tree
# drivers, and mkinitcpio all assume modules exist.
CONFIG_MODULES=y
CONFIG_MODULE_UNLOAD=y
CONFIG_MODVERSIONS=y
CONFIG_IKCONFIG=y
CONFIG_IKCONFIG_PROC=y

# ============== gaming / Proton / Wine ==============
# fsync/futex_waitv is what modern Proton uses for thread synchronisation.
CONFIG_FUTEX=y
CONFIG_FUTEX_PI=y
# Anonymous shared memory used by Wine and various anti-cheat-free titles.
CONFIG_MEMFD_CREATE=y
CONFIG_TRANSPARENT_HUGEPAGE=y
CONFIG_TRANSPARENT_HUGEPAGE_MADVISE=y
# 4K pages, pinned rather than inherited.
#
# arm64 defconfig already picks 4K, so this changes nothing today — it stops a
# future defconfig or a vendor fragment from quietly moving to 16K/64K, which
# would be very expensive to debug from the symptom. Emulator builds on ARM are
# page-size specific: ARMSX2 ships a distinct `Linux-arm64-4K-pages` AppImage,
# and the aarch64 RPCS3/Cemu/DuckStation builds assume 4K too. A 16K kernel does
# not fail to boot, it just breaks every emulator the user installs later, with
# nothing tying the two together.
#
# 4K is also what ALARM's stock aarch64 kernel uses (CONFIG_ARM64_4K_PAGES=y),
# so qemu-virt and ayn-odin3 stay comparable.
CONFIG_ARM64_4K_PAGES=y
# gamemode + scx schedulers.
# SCHED_CLASS_EXT depends on BPF_SYSCALL && BPF_JIT && DEBUG_INFO_BTF
# (kernel/Kconfig.preempt), and DEBUG_INFO_BTF needs `pahole` present at BUILD
# time — it is a toolchain dependency, not a config one. pahole is in the
# Dockerfile for exactly this reason.
#
# pahole alone is NOT enough, which cost two failed builds to work out. The full
# dependency chain from lib/Kconfig.debug in 7.2-rc7 is:
#
#   config DEBUG_INFO_BTF
#       depends on !DEBUG_INFO_SPLIT && !DEBUG_INFO_REDUCED
#       depends on BPF_SYSCALL
#       depends on PAHOLE_VERSION >= 122
#
# The blocker is DEBUG_INFO_REDUCED, which the arm64 defconfig sets. Note what is
# NOT the problem, because it is the obvious wrong guess: debug info is already
# on in the baseline — `make ARCH=arm64 defconfig` produces
#     CONFIG_DEBUG_INFO=y
#     CONFIG_DEBUG_INFO_DWARF_TOOLCHAIN_DEFAULT=y
#     CONFIG_DEBUG_INFO_REDUCED=y            <-- this is the one
# and pahole is present and detected (CONFIG_PAHOLE_VERSION=131 after
# olddefconfig, comfortably over the required 122).
#
# -g1-style reduced debug info omits the type information BTF is generated FROM,
# so pahole has nothing to convert and Kconfig disables BTF — taking
# SCHED_CLASS_EXT with it, since that depends on DEBUG_INFO_BTF via
# kernel/Kconfig.preempt. The symptom is two lines from the PKGBUILD's own check:
#     ::  NOT SET: CONFIG_DEBUG_INFO_BTF=y
#     ::  NOT SET: CONFIG_SCHED_CLASS_EXT=y
# neither of which names the symbol actually responsible.
#
# The DWARF choice below is therefore redundant with the defconfig — kept as an
# explicit pin, because "BTF needs real debug info" is the whole reason the
# REDUCED line above it exists and the two belong together.
#
# Cost: full debug info makes the build slower and vmlinux much larger, and
# embeds a few MB of BTF in the Image. Modules are stripped
# (INSTALL_MOD_STRIP=1) so the package stays reasonable and the 512 MiB boot
# partition has ample room. If that ever matters more than sched_ext does, the
# rollback is deleting the three DEBUG_INFO lines and SCHED_CLASS_EXT — nothing
# in this image requires scx today; it is here so scx-scheds is an install away
# rather than a kernel rebuild away.
CONFIG_BPF_SYSCALL=y
CONFIG_BPF_JIT=y
# CONFIG_DEBUG_INFO_REDUCED is not set
CONFIG_DEBUG_INFO_DWARF_TOOLCHAIN_DEFAULT=y
CONFIG_DEBUG_INFO_BTF=y
CONFIG_SCHED_CLASS_EXT=y

# ============== networking for NetworkManager + Steam ==============
CONFIG_NETFILTER=y
CONFIG_NF_CONNTRACK=m
CONFIG_NF_TABLES=m
CONFIG_NETFILTER_XTABLES=m
CONFIG_BRIDGE=m
CONFIG_TUN=m
CONFIG_VETH=m
CONFIG_CFG80211=m
CONFIG_MAC80211=m

# ============== misc userland expectations ==============
CONFIG_UNIX=y
CONFIG_UNIX_DIAG=m
CONFIG_INPUT_UINPUT=y
CONFIG_INPUT_JOYDEV=m
CONFIG_INPUT_EVDEV=y
CONFIG_HIDRAW=y
CONFIG_UHID=y
CONFIG_USB_HIDDEV=y
# CONFIG_JOYSTICK_XPAD moved to 25-controllers.config, where it is gated on
# CONFIG_INPUT_JOYSTICK. It was requested here and silently never applied.
CONFIG_SND_HDA_PREALLOC_SIZE=2048
CONFIG_UDMABUF=y
CONFIG_DMABUF_HEAPS=y
CONFIG_SYNC_FILE=y

# ============== pacman 7 sandbox ==============
# CONFIG_SECURITY_LANDLOCK is not optional on an Arch userland. pacman 7 sandboxes
# its downloader with Landlock and drops to the `alpm` user, and without kernel
# support it does not degrade — it REFUSES:
#
#     error: restricting filesystem access failed because Landlock is not
#            supported by the kernel!
#     error: switching to sandbox user 'alpm' failed!
#     error: failed to synchronize all databases
#
# So `pacman -Sy` and every install fails outright on the device. This is the one
# gap that made the custom kernel worse than ALARM's stock one, which has it.
#
# It went unnoticed because the BUILD works around the same problem from the other
# end: build_files/lib/pacman-sandbox.sh disables the sandbox inside Docker (no
# Landlock there either) and 50-configure.sh re-enables it in the shipped rootfs —
# correctly assuming the device kernel supports it. With linux-aarch64 it did.
#
# Only this one symbol is needed: it `select`s SECURITY_NETWORK and SECURITY_PATH
# itself, and `landlock` is already first in the default CONFIG_LSM string.
CONFIG_SECURITY_LANDLOCK=y
# Arch's default LSM set also carries yama (ptrace scope hardening). Cheap, and
# systemd-sysctl ships kernel.yama.ptrace_scope expecting it to exist.
CONFIG_SECURITY_YAMA=y

# ============== AppImages and disc images ==============
# An AppImage is a squashfs the runtime mounts through FUSE. XZ and ZSTD are
# already on above; these are the remaining pieces:
#   XATTR  executables inside an AppImage carry capabilities/xattrs, and mounting
#          without xattr support silently drops them
#   LZ4/LZO less common than zstd but used by some emulator AppImage builds, and
#          the failure is a mount that just refuses
CONFIG_SQUASHFS_XATTR=y
CONFIG_SQUASHFS_LZ4=y
CONFIG_SQUASHFS_LZO=y
# Emulator disc images: ISO9660 for CD-based systems, UDF for DVD. Modules, since
# nothing mounts them at boot.
CONFIG_ISO9660_FS=m
CONFIG_UDF_FS=m

# binfmt_misc, which systemd-binfmt expects to exist. Not needed by anything in
# the image TODAY — Steam's FEX lives inside Proton and needs no binfmt
# registration — but it is the prerequisite for ever running an x86-64 binary
# outside Steam, and it is what `systemd-binfmt.service` fails on otherwise.
CONFIG_BINFMT_MISC=y
